  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Lingering clouds and a few showers or a thunderstorm into \nThursday.\n\nA closed low will remain over the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic regions \nthrough tonight, then it will gradually shift eastward during \nThursday. This will take surface low pressure across southern New \nEngland this afternoon eventually offshore late tonight and Thursday.\nShortwave energy will continue to rotate around this closed low and \nacross our area through Thursday, with a stronger one sliding \nthrough tonight. The combination of weak instability with colder air \naloft and enough moisture and lift, should result in some showers \nsliding from north-northwest to south-southeast into this evening. A \nlow-topped thunderstorm cannot be ruled out. The greatest chances of \nthis occurring is roughly from near and north of I-78. While these \ntypically dissipate at night with the loss of heating, some showers \nmay linger through the overnight as a stronger shortwave moves \nacross the area. This should also keep cloud cover around through \ntonight. A gusty northwesterly breeze will diminish some tonight, \nthen increase again on Thursday. \n\nAs the closed low gradually shifts eastward during Thursday, a few \nshowers or a thunderstorm are still possible especially in the \nafternoon. This looks to be mostly from near and north/east of \nPhiladelphia. Given the continued cooler air aloft combined with \nboundary layer heating and cyclonic flow, a decent amount of cloud \ncover is anticipated for most of the area on Thursday. This will \nalso keep temperatures below average for this time of year. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A short period of tranquil weather for the end the \nweek and into Saturday before another system arrives Sunday.\n\nFollowing the departure of the recent storm up into Canada, high pressure\nbuilds back across the area Thursday night into Friday. The high weakens\nSaturday and is replaced by advancing low pressure from the Ohio\nValley area. This low strengthens and moves west of our area later\nSunday and into Monday. Thus system will bring increasing chances\nfor showers and a few tstms Sunday afternoon for the western counties\nand all areas Sunday night and Monday. The PWAT values off the forecast\nsoundings during these times show more 2+ inch PWAT values for our\nregion. So it's possible that excessive rainfall may develop for\nparts of the region.",
